<p>Anthropic published a new set of measurements on September 17, 2026 to show how much AI is involved in building AI. The headline figure is that Claude now “leads” 26% of Anthropic’s measured AI research and development work as of August 2026. That number is useful, but it does not mean Claude is independently building 26% of the next model. The measurement is about how work is performed, under a defined automation scale and with human oversight.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/anthropic-ai-rd-automation-index-26-percent.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Anthropic R&D Automation Index showing AI collaboration and leadership levels"></figure>
<h2>What the 26% figure measures</h2>
<p>Anthropic built a prototype called the Anthropic R&D Automation Index. It catalogs types of AI research and development work, rates how automated each task is, and aggregates those ratings. The framework uses an Automation Level scale from AL0 to AL5. AL0 means no AI involvement. AL3 describes AI collaborating on large parts of a task under close human direction. AL4 means AI “leads” most of a task end-to-end from a high-level prompt while a human supervises. AL5 is full autonomy without a human in the loop.</p>
<p>Anthropic says Claude is not fully autonomous for any measured subset of AI R&D. At the same time, Claude leads 26% of the measured work, and more than 90% is at or above the “AI collaborates” level. The company also says the 26% share was below 1% in February 2026, showing how quickly internal AI assistance has grown in its own reporting.</p>
<h2>Why “leads” does not mean “works alone”</h2>
<p>The word “leads” can sound stronger than the measurement itself. Under the published definition, an AL4 task can still include human supervision. The useful interpretation is that the model can complete most of the task from a high-level request, while a human remains responsible for oversight.</p>
<p>That distinction matters for software development, evaluation, research analysis and other technical work. A model can do large amounts of execution while a researcher still decides which question to ask, checks important outputs, handles exceptions and approves consequential actions.</p>
<h2>Anthropic is also measuring agent oversight</h2>
<p>The same publication tracks how internal research and engineering agents are monitored. Anthropic says roughly 30,000 agents were doing research and engineering work at one time on its most-used internal platform in August 2026. All of those actions passed through an online monitor before execution, while the same activity was also ingested by an offline monitoring system for later review.</p>
<p>Anthropic reports that, across more than one billion decisions analyzed in August, about 0.002% were blocked by the online monitor. The company says offline monitoring flags roughly one to two transcripts in every thousand for additional review, with a smaller set escalated to human reviewers.</p>
<h2>What makes the measurement hard to compare</h2>
<p>Anthropic itself highlights two limits. First, there is no shared industry methodology for measuring AI-led R&D. A 26% figure from one lab is not automatically comparable with a number produced under a different task taxonomy or rating system. Second, Anthropic uses its own models to evaluate some of its systems, which creates the possibility that the judging model makes the same kind of mistake as the system being evaluated.</p>
<p>Anthropic says independent third-party evaluators could make these measurements more useful across companies. It also proposes publishing coverage, review latency and escalation rates so oversight can be tracked alongside automation.</p>
<h2>What the metric means for AI teams</h2>
<p>Companies do not need a frontier-lab research program to borrow the idea. A smaller internal index can track where AI assists work and where humans still need to direct or review it.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Simple question to track</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Automation level</td><td>How much of the task can AI complete?</td></tr><tr><td>Human review</td><td>Which outputs must a person approve?</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>What share of AI activity is monitored?</td></tr><tr><td>Escalation rate</td><td>How often does activity need deeper review?</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>For production agents, this gives teams a better picture than a simple count of prompts or tokens. It separates usage from actual changes in how work is done.</p>
<h2>How to avoid overreading the trend</h2>
<p>Anthropic’s result is a snapshot of its own internal work, measured with its own task catalog and methodology. It is evidence about one company’s process, not a universal measure of how fast all AI development is becoming automated. The figure also does not show that the next frontier model can build itself without people.</p>
<p>A useful way to follow the trend is to watch the same metric over time, keep the task definitions stable, publish the methodology, and add independent checks where possible. That makes a time series more useful than a single headline percentage. Teams also looking at deployment choices can pair this measurement with the <a href="/claude/claude-aws-deployment-options-2026">Claude on AWS deployment options guide</a> when deciding how much work to automate and where human review should remain.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Does 26% mean Claude is building the next model by itself?</h3><p>No. Anthropic says Claude is not fully autonomous for any measured subset of its AI R&D. The 26% figure refers to work where Claude can lead most of a task under human supervision.</p><h3>What is the Anthropic R&D Automation Index?</h3><p>It is a prototype index that catalogs AI R&D tasks, rates their level of automation and aggregates those ratings to estimate how much AI is doing at different stages of the work.</p><h3>Can other AI labs use the same idea?</h3><p>Yes. Anthropic says other frontier developers could publish similar measurements, especially if they use clear methods and independent verification so results can be compared more reliably.</p>
